---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';

/**
 * SEO / compatibility page (research Part 12 P7, spec section 4 of badger-fit
 * docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-18-m-web-4-orphaned-pages.md).
 *
 * TRADEMARK HYGIENE, binding: StrongLifts is someone else's mark. Everything on
 * this page is phrased as compatibility (Badger imports their CSV; Badger ships
 * a 5x5 template written for Badger), never as affiliation, partnership, or
 * endorsement. The closing note says so in words. Do not reword the title, the
 * h1, or the note into anything that reads as "the StrongLifts app for X".
 *
 * Every capability claim here was checked against the app source on 2026-07-25,
 * app version 1.1.1: the seeded template contents (app_database.dart
 * _seedProgramsV24), the suggestion-only progressive overload
 * (progressive_overload_provider.dart), the deload behavior (features/deload/),
 * plateau detection defaults, and the Stronglifts CSV parser's actual column
 * coverage (stronglifts_parser.dart). The "what Badger does not do" section is
 * the load-bearing half of the page: it is what makes the rest believable to
 * someone arriving from an app that did run the program for them.
 */

const description =
  'Run a 5x5 in Badger and bring your StrongLifts history with you. What the built-in template does, what you still decide yourself, and exactly what the CSV import carries across.';
---

<HelpLayout title="StrongLifts 5x5 tracker" description={description}>
  <ArticleSchema
    slot="head"
    headline="StrongLifts 5x5 in Badger"
    description={description}
    dateModified="2026-09-05"
    about={['StrongLifts 5x5', '5x5 strength training']}
  />

  <h1>StrongLifts 5x5 in Badger</h1>

  <p>
    Two things usually bring people to this page: they want to keep running 5x5 without paying a
    subscription for the privilege, and they do not want to abandon the history they already have.
    Badger does both. It is a log with tools rather than an app that runs the program for you, and
    that difference is worth understanding before you switch.
  </p>

  <h2 id="whats-in-the-box">What Badger brings to a 5x5</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The template ships with the app.</strong> Under Routines, in the Beginner Programs
      folder, there is a StrongLifts 5x5 routine with two alternating days. Workout A is squat, bench
      press, and barbell row, five sets of five each. Workout B is squat and overhead press, five
      sets of five, plus one set of five deadlifts. Set it active and it is ready.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It alternates for you.</strong> The two days are deliberately not pinned to weekdays,
      so the Today screen leads with whichever comes next based on what you last did. You never have
      to remember whether today is an A or a B.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your weights, not ours.</strong> The template carries the rep scheme and no weights at
      all. The first session is you entering what you actually lift; after that each session starts
      prefilled from the last one.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A rest timer that starts itself.</strong> It runs when you complete a working set, two
      minutes by default, and every exercise can carry its own. Squats and rows want different rests,
      and 5x5 is mostly an argument about resting long enough.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A plate calculator</strong> that works from the plates your gym actually owns, in
      barbell, machine, or single-lever modes, and tells you what goes on each side.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A warmup generator</strong> that builds the ramp up to your work weight and rounds every
      rung down to a load you can really make with your plates.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>PRs and graphs on every lift</strong>, with no cap on how many routines you keep or how
      far back your history goes.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-you-decide">What you still decide yourself</h2>

  <p>
    If you are coming from an app whose whole job was to run the program, this is the part that
    decides whether Badger suits you.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Badger does not add the weight for you.</strong> There is no setting that puts 2.5 kg on
      the bar every session. What it does instead: once you have held the same top weight for three
      sessions with every working set finished, a line appears above the entry row reading
      <em>Suggested: 82.5 kg</em>. You type it in, or you do not. The trigger count is adjustable in
      settings.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>There is an optional write-back, and it is conservative.</strong> Auto-progress routine
      weights, off unless you switch it on, offers at the end of a workout to save weights you already
      lifted above the plan back into the routine. It never invents an increment, and it asks per
      exercise before it writes anything.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No "fail three times, drop ten percent" rule.</strong> Badger has a deload feature, off
      by default, and it works on a different idea: it schedules a whole light week starting the
      following Monday, at half the weight with two fewer working sets (the defaults; both are
    adjustable), which you confirm or decline
      when you start that session. It is a program-wide light week, not a per-lift reset triggered by
      missed reps. Cutting one stalled lift by ten percent is something you do by hand.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing decides you have stalled and moves you on.</strong> Plateau detection is
      optional and off by default, applies only to weight-and-reps exercises, and needs at least five
      sessions spanning three weeks of a flat estimated 1RM before it says anything. When it does, it
      is a banner with three suggestions, not a decision taken for you.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No watch app</strong> on either platform, and no logging from your wrist.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No sync between devices.</strong> Your training lives on the phone you log it on. There
      is an optional backup to a private folder in your own Google Drive, and a plain database file
      you can move yourself, but two phones do not stay in step. See
      <a href="/help/phone-migration">moving to a new phone</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <span class="caps">The short version</span>
    <p>
      The honest summary: Badger will hold the program, remember your weights, time your rests, and
      show you the graph. Deciding when to add weight, when to repeat, and when to back off stays
      yours. Plenty of people prefer that. If you wanted the app to make those calls, it is better to
      know now.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="import">Bringing your StrongLifts history over</h2>

  <p>
    Badger reads the CSV that StrongLifts exports, so the years you already logged come with you and
    your PRs and graphs start out full rather than empty.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>In StrongLifts, go to <strong>Settings &rarr; Export workout data</strong> to produce a CSV.</li>
    <li>
      Get the file onto the phone you train with, however you normally move a file: email it to
      yourself, drop it in cloud storage, use a cable.
    </li>
    <li>
      In Badger, go to <strong>Settings &rarr; Your data &rarr; Import from Stronglifts</strong> and
      pick the file. This is also offered during first-launch onboarding.
    </li>
    <li>Check the preview: exercises, workout days, sets, and the detected weight unit.</li>
    <li>Import.</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>What carries across:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>Every set, with its date, exercise, weight, and reps, read straight from the per-set columns.</li>
    <li>
      Kilograms or pounds, detected from the file's own headers, and stored in the unit you logged
      them in. Nothing is converted or rounded on the way in.
    </li>
    <li>The workout name becomes a note on that session, and a row's note attaches to its first set.</li>
    <li>
      PRs are recalculated from the imported history on their own, and new exercises land in an
      "Imported" category so you can find and re-sort them.
    </li>
    <li>
      When each session started and how long it ran, read from the export's own time columns, so
      imported history shows real durations.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>What does not, so nothing surprises you later:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Warmups.</strong> The StrongLifts export does not mark them, so every logged set arrives
      as a working set. There is no way for the importer to guess.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Failed sets, as failures.</strong> A missed set of five comes in as what you actually
      did. Three reps is three reps; nothing labels it a miss.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Which program a session belonged to.</strong> The import brings history, not the
      program. The 5x5 template is a separate thing you set active.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The import runs entirely on your phone, straight from the file you picked, and you can run it more
    than once, so a second or partial export is fine to add later. Nothing already logged is
    overwritten: sets landing on a date you already have merge into that session. Full detail in
    <a href="/help/getting-started#importing-stronglifts">importing from Stronglifts</a> and
    <a href="/guides/switching">switching from another app</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-file">The template as a file you can read</h2>

  <p>
    The same 5x5 routine is also published as a download:
    <a href="/programs">stronglifts-5x5.badger</a> on the program gallery, exported from the app
    itself. Open it on your phone and Badger imports it. Open it in a text editor and it is ordinary
    readable text, so you can see exactly what you are agreeing to before you import it, or edit it first. The
    <a href="/badger-format">file format</a> is documented for the same reason.
  </p>

  <h2 id="after-5x5">When 5x5 runs out</h2>

  <p>
    Linear progression ends for everybody, usually within months, and that moment is when most people
    reconsider their whole app. Worth knowing where Badger stands before you get there rather than
    after.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Stretch it first.</strong> Smaller jumps, a light week, and rests long enough to be
      boring will all buy time. Badger has the tools for each; none of them is automatic.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>GZCLP and Upper/Lower</strong> both ship as templates in the app and download as files
      from the <a href="/programs">program gallery</a>. Either is a reasonable next step.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>5/3/1 and other percentage programs are a real limit.</strong> Badger ships a Boring But
      Big skeleton, but it will not compute working weights as percentages of a training max, so the
      arithmetic each cycle is yours. That is written up plainly on the
      <a href="/programs">program gallery</a>, together with a way to say you want it built, which is
      the only signal this site collects.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="cost">What it costs</h2>

  <p>
    Nothing. No account, no subscription, no ads, no cap on routines or history, and exporting your
    own numbers is not a paid feature. Badger is free on Google Play, with an iOS beta open in
    TestFlight. If the reason you are shopping is that a tracker you already paid for changed the
    deal, read <a href="/promise">the promise</a>, which is the version of that written down where you
    can hold us to it, and
    <a href="/guides/workout-tracker-no-subscription">the no-subscription guide</a>, which compares
    what free actually means across the trackers people usually weigh up.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trademark">About the name</h2>

  <p class="fineprint">
    StrongLifts is a trademark of its owner. Badger is not affiliated with, endorsed by, sponsored by,
    or connected to StrongLifts in any way. This page describes compatibility and nothing else: Badger
    reads the CSV file that their app exports, and the 5x5 routine that ships inside Badger was
    written for Badger by us, from the publicly known shape of the program. If you want their app,
    they are easy to find.
  </p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  /* The trademark note is fine print by intent: it is a disclaimer, and it should
     read as one rather than competing with the guidance above it. Muted is
     AA-checked against the page ground in both themes, so it stays readable. */
  .fineprint {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
